## 1. The Core Formulas and Symbolic Syntax
To systematically audit network layouts and detect hidden infrastructure vulnerabilities, we map the entire network architecture into formal graph data structures.
## Graph Mathematical Definitions

* The Network Architecture Matrix ($G = (V, E)$): A directed graph where $V$ represents the set of all computing nodes (Frontend proxies, API gateways, load balancers, internal databases) and $E$ represents the set of all active network communication channels or API routes connecting them.
* In-Degree and Out-Degree ($deg^-(v) / deg^+(v)$): For any given server node $v$, its in-degree counts the number of incoming request channels hitting it, and its out-degree counts how many backend systems it communicates with. High-value internal endpoints (like hidden database wrappers) typically have low in-degree from the public web but high connection densities internally.
* Strongly Connected Components (SCC): A subset of server vertices where every node is reachable from every other node in the subset. A secure architecture mandates that backend core storage clusters are completely isolated from the public internet routing graph, meaning no path exists from the public entry node ($v_0$) to the private component group ($V_{\text{core}}$):
$$\forall v \in V_{\text{core}}, \quad \text{Path}(v_0 \to v) = \emptyset$$ 

