## 2. Step-by-Step System Testing Using Network Graph Mapping
Let’s use Graph Theory to map and solve a classic architectural breakdown: Unauthenticated Routing Exposure via API Gateway Misconfigurations.
The Scenario: A betting site uses a front-facing reverse proxy proxy-node ($v_{\text{proxy}}$) to filter and route traffic. Legitimate user traffic hitting the site is mapped through a chain of routing nodes.

* $v_0$: Public Web Traffic
* $v_1$: Frontend Gateway Router (/)
* $v_2$: Main User Interface API (/api/v1/)
* $v_3$: Internal Admin Inventory Cluster (/api/internal/) - Intended to be inaccessible to $v_0$

The developer attempts to write a path verification policy configuration matching this intended routing matrix:

# Broken proxy configuration
location /api/v1/ {
    proxy_pass http://backend_user_cluster/;
}
location /api/internal/ {
    allow 10.0.0.0/8; # Trust zone restriction
    deny all;
}

## The Path Traversal Graph Anomaly
Let's model the URI path parser engine using an Adjacency Matrix Mapping Strategy. An attacker discovers a parsing discrepancy called Nginx Alias Traversal or Path Normalization Mismatch. If the gateway configuration has loose boundary validations, sending a path string containing dot-dot-slash sequence combinations alters the graph traversal execution flow on the backend.
When an unauthenticated visitor issues a targeted request to:
https://target.com
The frontend proxy node reads the path string literally without resolving the relative path tokens. It matches the string prefix to the rule /api/v1/ and routes the traffic directly into the backend cluster network wrapper.
Once the packet crosses the network perimeter firewall and lands on the backend processing server, the backend application evaluates the string using standard OS filesystem path normalization mathematics:
$$\text{Path Normalization: } \text{"/api/v1/../internal/"} \implies \text{"/api/internal/"}$$ 
## The Graph Collapse
By exploiting the differences in how the two independent server layers parse data strings, the attacker has created a hidden pathway in the network graph:
$$v_0 \to v_1 \to v_3$$ 
The packet bypasses the security rule assigned to the /api/internal/ gateway node entirely because the traffic entered through the approved /api/v1/ network node channel first. This allows the attacker to dump internal API schemas without an account.
